@node Palette Selection
@section §11.4 — Palette Selection

@itemize
@item
@strong{Modern is the default.} An artifact that declares nothing uses
@code{steelbore}. No declaration is required to be compliant.
@item
@strong{One palette per project.} A project @strong{authors} against
exactly one palette, declares it in its @file{README.md} beside the §5.2
posture section, and registers the theme under that slug. That palette is
the project's @strong{default} (§11.6.3), not the only theme it may
render: §11.6.1 requires a project to register the family so a system or
user declaration can be answered. Tokens from two palettes are never
@strong{combined in one interface} --- the contrast guarantees are
computed per-palette and do not survive mixing. Replacing one palette's
tokens with another's, whole-surface and at once, is not combining;
§11.6.2 governs it.
@item
@strong{Every palette ships its high-contrast sibling.} The §18.1
accessible-mode toggle selects
@code{<palette-slug>-high-contrast}; @code{steelbore-mono} is
palette-independent and serves them all.
@item
@strong{A project may declare a light/dark pair.} @code{steelbore} is the
family's dark default and @code{steelbore-navywhite} its light one ---
§11.3.4 is the only light-canvas member. A project may name its own pair
in @file{README.md}, or declare @code{light = none} with a stated reason
to ship dark-only. §11.6.2 defines how the pair is used.
@item
@strong{The canvas is mandatory within its palette.} Substituting a
different background for a declared palette is non-compliant. The rule
binds whichever palette is @emph{in force}: when §11.6.2 switches
palettes, the new palette's canvas comes with it, unaltered.
@item
@strong{Values are read, never retyped.} The canonical machine-readable
source for every palette, variant, and contrast matrix is
@code{steelbore.toml}, shipped by the @code{steelbore-color-palette}
skill. Where this document and that file disagree, this document
governs and the file is corrected. A theme registry supplied by the
operating system (§11.6.4) is advisory and never authoritative; the
application's own copy governs.
@item
Documents, editor themes, and terminal themes follow the project's
declared palette. Where no project context exists (a standalone
document, a one-off diagram), use Modern.
@item
@strong{Fidelity palettes are not adoptable.} The §11.5 palettes are
registered for interoperability and are excluded from this section --- a
project may not declare one as its palette. They also ship no
high-contrast sibling, so the bullet above does not apply to them.
@item
@strong{Palettes have reference names.} Every palette carries a
reference name (§11.4.1) alongside its slug. The slug stays the machine
identifier; the reference name is for prose and conversation.
@item
@strong{The registered set is not the declared palette.} What a project
ships in its theme registry, and how a running application chooses among
those themes, are §11.6.
@end itemize

@subsection §11.4.1 — Reference Names

Each palette carries a @strong{reference name} for prose, conversation, and
documentation. Reference names are @emph{additive}: the slug remains the
machine identifier that themes, @code{steelbore.toml} keys, and application
theme lookups use, and it is unchanged by this section. Both forms appear in
the canonical file, the reference name as the @code{reference} key of each
@code{[palettes.<slug>]} table.

