@node System Theme
@section §11.6 — System Theme Declaration & Resolution

§11.1 says palette references go through a named theme. §11.4 says a
project declares one palette. Neither says what happens when the
@emph{machine} has an opinion --- and Steelbore OS does. This section
closes that gap from both sides: what an application ships and how it
decides which theme to render, and how an operating system declares the
theme it wants applications to follow.

@strong{Scope.} This section binds every application published under the
two namespaces §6.4 authorizes --- @code{github.com/Spacecraft-Software}
and @code{github.com/UnbreakableMJ} --- in every class that draws a user
interface: @strong{GUI, TUI, and CLI alike}. A CLI is not exempt. It
already honors @code{NO_COLOR} (§18.2.1) and already emits ANSI color, so
it already has a theme whether or not it names one. A library, a build
plugin, or any artifact with no user-facing output has no theme to
resolve and records §11.6 as N/A. @strong{Projects registered as games
are not exempt} --- §18.5 carves out §18 and §10, not §11 --- but a game
satisfies this section in its @strong{menus, HUD, and settings chrome};
the simulated world it renders is not an interface surface under §11.

@subsection §11.6.1 — Shipping the family

A project @strong{authors} against one palette and @strong{registers}
several. These are different obligations, and §11.4's one-palette rule
governs only the first.

The @strong{registered set} is the thirteen themes that bind the §11.1
eleven-role contract:

@multitable {@strong{MUST NOT register}} {The six conforming palettes --- @code{steelbore}, @code{steelbore-blue}} {The smallest set that answers every §11.6.3 outcome without falling back}
@headitem
Obligation
 @tab Themes
 @tab Why
@item
@strong{MUST register}
 @tab The six conforming palettes --- @code{steelbore},
@code{steelbore-blue}, @code{steelbore-blackpinkpanther},
@code{steelbore-matrixgreen}, @code{steelbore-navywhite},
@code{tokyonight} --- each with its @code{-high-contrast} sibling, plus
@code{steelbore-mono}. Thirteen themes
 @tab Every one binds the same eleven role tokens, so a theme layer that
reads @code{steelbore.toml} registers them in a loop. A declaration can
then always be answered
@item
@strong{MAY register}
 @tab @code{steelbore-classic} and
@code{steelbore-classic-high-contrast}
 @tab Classic keeps the legacy six-role contract (§11.2) and defines no
surface class, so it is registrable only by an application that
implements that contract as well
@item
@strong{MUST NOT register}
 @tab A §11.5 fidelity palette, other than as an explicitly
user-selectable extra
 @tab §11.5 bars adoption, and this section is not a route around it
@end multitable

Three of the thirteen were already required --- the project's own palette
by §11.4, its @code{-high-contrast} sibling by §11.1.1, and
@code{steelbore-mono} by §11.1.1 --- so this section adds ten themes, all
of them already written out in @code{steelbore.toml}.

@strong{Registering is not defaulting.} The default remains the project's
§11.4 palette (§11.6.3, source 5). Registering the family makes a
declaration @emph{answerable}; it does not change what renders when
nothing is declared.

@subsection §11.6.2 — Light and dark

Every conforming palette but one is dark-canvas;
@code{steelbore-navywhite} (§11.3.4) is the family's only light-canvas
member. A platform light preference therefore cannot be answered by
lifting a token --- it can only be answered by rendering a different
palette, and §11.4 has to be read precisely for that to be legal.

@strong{Switching is not mixing.} §11.4 forbids @emph{combining} tokens
from two palettes in one interface, because the contrast guarantees are
computed per-palette and do not survive mixing. A color-scheme switch
combines nothing: the whole token set is replaced at once, the new
palette's canvas comes with it unaltered, and no frame ever carries a
token from two palettes. Switching is accordingly compliant, and mixing
remains forbidden. Two rules follow, and they are hard:

@itemize
@item
@strong{The switch is atomic and whole-surface.} A partially re-themed
interface --- one panel light and another dark, or a canvas cached from
the previous palette --- is non-compliant. An application that cannot
re-theme atomically MUST resolve once at startup and hold until it is
restarted, rather than switch a surface at a time.
@item
@strong{The canvas travels with the palette.} §11.4's mandatory-canvas
rule binds whichever palette is in force. Substituting a light
background under a dark palette is the non-compliance §11.4 already
names; it is not a light mode.
@end itemize

A project MAY declare a @strong{light/dark pair} in its @file{README.md}
beside its §11.4 palette declaration. Where it declares none, the family
defaults apply:

@multitable {Platform preference} {Source 4 does not fire --- resolution falls through} {Source 4 does not fire --- resolution falls through}
@headitem
Platform preference
 @tab Family default
 @tab With a declared pair
@item
Prefer dark
 @tab @code{steelbore}
 @tab The project's declared dark member
@item
Prefer light
 @tab @code{steelbore-navywhite}
 @tab The project's declared light counterpart
@item
No preference
 @tab Source 4 does not fire --- resolution falls through
 @tab Source 4 does not fire --- resolution falls through
@end multitable

@itemize
@item
@strong{A dark-only project declares it.} A project whose identity does
not survive a light canvas MAY declare @code{light = none} and state the
reason in @file{README.md} --- a documented exception, not a free choice,
on the same footing as §14.2.1. It then ignores a platform light
preference and reports that under @code{--verbose}. It does @emph{not}
get to ignore §18: high contrast and @code{steelbore-mono} still apply.
@item
@strong{Solarized is not the pair it looks like.} Solarized Dark and
Solarized Light (§11.5) are a natural pair upstream, and §11.5 bars both
from adoption --- so neither may ever be the target of source 4. Where a
user has selected one at source 1 in an application that offers it, that
selection stands; it is a user preference, not conformance.
@item
Each palette's canvas polarity and its counterpart are recorded in
@code{steelbore.toml}. They are read, never retyped (§11.4).
@end itemize

@subsection §11.6.3 — Resolution order

The theme resolves at startup in two stages: a @strong{base palette},
then a @strong{variant overlay}. Keeping them separate is what makes this
section composable --- §18.1 and @code{NO_COLOR} choose a sibling, never
a palette, so an accessibility signal can never silently change the
brand.

@strong{Stage 1 --- the base palette.} Precedence, highest first:

@multitable {@strong{4. Platform color scheme}} {@code{SPACECRAFT_THEME=<slug>} --- the umbrella-wide environment variable, carrying a slug}
@headitem
Source
 @tab Form
@item
@strong{1. In-app selection}
 @tab @code{--theme=<slug>}, or @code{[theme] name = "<slug>"} in the
project's config file
@item
@strong{2. Environment}
 @tab @code{SPACECRAFT_THEME=<slug>} --- the umbrella-wide environment
variable, carrying a slug
@item
@strong{3. System declaration}
 @tab The @code{active} key of the highest-precedence §11.6.4
declaration file
@item
@strong{4. Platform color scheme}
 @tab The platform's light/dark preference, mapped through §11.6.2
@item
@strong{5. Project default}
 @tab The project's declared §11.4 palette --- @code{steelbore} where it
declares none
@end multitable

@itemize
@item
@strong{An unusable slug is skipped, never fatal.} A slug that is not a
conforming member of the family, or that the application has not
registered (§11.6.1), is discarded and resolution continues at the next
lower source. A theme is never rendered partially, and a typo in
@file{/etc} never leaves a machine without a working interface (§3.1 ---
graceful degradation).
@item
@strong{Fidelity slugs resolve only at source 1.} A §11.5 slug is honored
where a user selects it inside an application that offers it, and is
ignored at sources 2, 3, and 4 --- §11.5 bars adoption, and a system
declaration is adoption.
@item
@strong{Source 4 is graphical only.} A terminal exposes no portable
color-scheme query, and probing for one is neither required nor relied
upon. For CLI and TUI applications source 4 is simply absent --- which is
precisely why source 3 exists.
@item
The resolved theme, the deciding stage-1 source, and the stage-2 overlay
MUST be reported under @code{--verbose}, in the same spirit as §18.1: a
user can always tell why the interface looks the way it does.
@end itemize

@strong{Stage 2 --- the variant overlay.} The overlay chooses which
sibling of the stage-1 palette renders. It never changes the palette.

@multitable {@strong{4. Platform high contrast}} {@code{<base>-high-contrast}} {Mono is the only variant that surrenders color outright, so it outranks high contrast}
@headitem
Signal
 @tab Renders
 @tab Note
@item
@strong{1. Pinned variant}
 @tab As named
 @tab The user named a @code{-high-contrast} or @code{-mono} slug at
stage-1 source 1 or 2. An explicit pin outranks every inferred signal
@item
@strong{2. @code{NO_COLOR}}
 @tab @code{steelbore-mono}
 @tab Mono is the only variant that surrenders color outright, so it
outranks high contrast: a user who asked for no color may not be handed a
colored sheet
@item
@strong{3. §18.1 accessible mode}
 @tab @code{<base>-high-contrast}
 @tab Resolved by §18.1, whose four-source precedence this section
neither restates nor modifies
@item
@strong{4. Platform high contrast}
 @tab @code{<base>-high-contrast}
 @tab The user has already expressed the preference system-wide, so it is
read from the platform independently of the §18.1 toggle (§18.3)
@item
@strong{5. None of the above}
 @tab The stage-1 palette itself
 @tab The §11.1.1 default, unchanged
@end multitable

@itemize
@item
@code{NO_COLOR} is @strong{a color instruction, not an accessibility
instruction}. It selects @code{steelbore-mono} whether or not it also
enabled accessible mode as a §18.1 source-4 hint, and
@code{SPACECRAFT_A11Y=0} does not undo it --- turning accessible mode off
says nothing about wanting color back.
@item
@code{steelbore-mono} is palette-independent (§11.1.1), so under it the
stage-1 palette does not render. It is still resolved, and still reported
under @code{--verbose}.
@item
A declaration file's @code{high-contrast} key enters at signal 4. It
selects a @strong{theme sibling only} and MUST NOT be read as enabling
accessible mode: §18.1 is the only switch for that, and §18's behavioral
requirements are not a color setting.
@end itemize

@subsection §11.6.4 — The system declaration

Steelbore OS MUST provide a way to declare the active theme that a
@strong{running} application can read. The mechanism is a plain file, and
that is deliberate: it has to work for a CLI in a text console --- no
session bus, no desktop portal, no daemon, no D-Bus name to acquire.

@strong{Search path}, highest precedence first:

@multitable {@code{$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/steelbore/theme.toml}} {Per-user declaration. @code{$XDG_CONFIG_HOME} defaults to @file{~/.config} when unset}
@headitem
Path
 @tab Role
@item
@code{$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/steelbore/theme.toml}
 @tab Per-user declaration. @code{$XDG_CONFIG_HOME} defaults to
@file{~/.config} when unset
@item
@file{/etc/steelbore/theme.toml}
 @tab System declaration, written by the OS configuration
@end multitable

Keys are read from the highest-precedence file that supplies them: a
per-user file setting only @code{active} does not erase the system file's
@code{light} and @code{dark}. @strong{The absence of both files is no
declaration}, and resolution moves to source 4. A file present with
@code{active = "steelbore"} @emph{is} a declaration --- of Modern,
explicitly --- and the distinction matters, because a system may need to
pin Modern rather than inherit whatever the default later becomes.

@strong{Schema.} TOML, because @code{steelbore.toml} already is TOML and
no consumer should need a second parser:

@verbatim
# /etc/steelbore/theme.toml -- The Steelbore Standard, section 11.6
[theme]
active              = "steelbore"            # required: a conforming slug
dark                = "steelbore"            # optional: the dark member
light               = "steelbore-navywhite"  # optional: the light member
follow-color-scheme = true                   # optional, default true
high-contrast       = false                  # optional, default false

[meta]
standard = "11.6"                            # the clause this file targets
source   = "bravais"                         # optional: what wrote it
@end verbatim

@itemize
@item
@strong{The declaration carries slugs, never colors.} An operating system
declares @emph{which} theme; it never supplies the hex values. Values come
from @code{steelbore.toml} by way of the application's own copy (§11.4).
This is the same division §13 draws for component systems: the platform
chooses the vocabulary, it never chooses the colors.
@item
An OS MAY additionally install a resolved registry at
@file{/etc/steelbore/themes.json} for tooling. It is @strong{advisory}: an
application MUST NOT require it, and where it disagrees with the
application's own @code{steelbore.toml}, the application's copy governs.
@item
@code{follow-color-scheme = false} disables source 4 for that scope, so a
declared theme survives whatever the desktop's appearance setting says.
@item
An unparseable file, or one carrying keys a reader does not know, is
@strong{ignored with a warning} and the next source decides. Never fatal.
@code{[meta] standard} lets a reader recognize a file written against a
later revision and fall back conservatively rather than guess.
@item
The environment variable is @code{SPACECRAFT_THEME}, in the same
umbrella-wide @code{SPACECRAFT_} namespace as §18.1's
@code{SPACECRAFT_A11Y}, and it carries a @strong{slug}.
@code{STEELBORE_THEME} is @strong{not} a Standard interface and MUST NOT
be given slug semantics: it is already in use as a boolean shell flag,
and overloading it would make @code{STEELBORE_THEME=true} resolve to a
theme that does not exist and fall through silently.
@item
Per-role color environment variables (@code{SPACECRAFT_BACKGROUND} and
the like) are @strong{not} a Standard interface. A conforming application
reads role values from @code{steelbore.toml}, not from the environment. An
OS may export them for shell scripts and non-conforming third-party
programs; nothing in §11 depends on them.
@end itemize

@subsection §11.6.5 — Obligations on the declaring side

The requirement is symmetrical: an operating system that ships §11 themes
ships the declaration too. Steelbore OS --- in every flavor, NixOS
(Bravais), GNU Guix System (Ginx), or any successor --- MUST:

@itemize
@item
Render §11.6.4's system declaration from its own theme selection, so the
one word that themes the machine is also the word applications read. The
declaration is generated, never hand-maintained alongside the selection.
@item
Export @code{SPACECRAFT_THEME} into the session environment, so a process
that never reads a file still resolves correctly at source 2. The export
must reach graphical sessions and system services, not login shells
alone.
@item
Keep the platform's color-scheme preference @strong{in agreement} with
the declared palette's polarity. A system declaring a light-canvas
palette while telling toolkits to prefer dark is internally inconsistent,
and third-party applications --- which read only the platform preference
--- will get it wrong.
@item
Validate the slug at configuration-evaluation time, so an unknown theme
fails the build rather than the boot.
@end itemize

An OS that also offers a theme-switching command SHOULD write the
per-user declaration (@code{$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/steelbore/theme.toml}) as
well as its own build-time selection, so a switch takes effect for
conforming applications without a rebuild.

