@end ifnottex

@node Assurance Categories
@chapter §19 — Assurance Categories, Tailoring & Conformance

§3 fixes the priority order for every artifact; it does not say how much
@emph{evidence} a given artifact owes. A bootloader that leaves a machine
unbootable and a colour-palette skill that renders a swatch wrong are both
governed by this standard, and until now both were governed identically.
Uniform obligation on unequal consequence produces one of two failures: the
small artifact carries ceremony it does not need, or the critical one carries
no more assurance than the small one. This chapter grades the obligation.

@strong{Provenance.} This chapter and §20 through §26 are informed by
ISO/IEC/IEEE 29148:2018 (requirements engineering) and ECSS-E-ST-40C (ESA/ECSS
space software engineering), credited per §15.3. No text from either document is
reproduced here; the categories, thresholds, gate structure, and wording are
Spacecraft Software's own, and where the two sources assume a
customer/supplier contract with a review board, this standard substitutes what
a single maintainer can actually execute.

@menu
* Category Definitions::        §19.1 — The four assurance categories.
* Category Declaration::        §19.2 — Declaring and changing a category.
* Graded Obligations::          §19.3 — What each category owes.
* Engineering Gates::           §19.4 — The three gates.
* Tailoring Register::          §19.5 — Recorded tailoring.
* Conformance Claim::           §19.6 — Claiming conformance.
@end menu

@node Category Definitions
@section §19.1 — The Four Assurance Categories

Category is assigned by @strong{consequence of failure}, never by effort,
line count, or how interesting the project is.

@multitable {@strong{C --- Standard}} {Failure destroys or corrupts data irrecoverably, prevents a machine from booting, or breaches a security or privacy boundary. Zamak, Pearlite, Cypher/Adit, Vault, and any crypto, bootloader, package-installation, or credential-handling path.}
@headitem
Category
 @tab Failure consequence and typical projects
@item
@strong{A --- Critical}
 @tab Failure destroys or corrupts data irrecoverably, prevents a machine
from booting, or breaches a security or privacy boundary. Bootloaders,
declarative system managers, credential stores, SSH and cryptographic
transports, package installation paths.
@item
@strong{B --- Significant}
 @tab Failure loses unsaved work, corrupts a user artifact recoverably, or
silently produces a wrong answer the user relies on. Editors, package
managers, conversion and tracing engines, and any tool whose output is
consumed without inspection.
@item
@strong{C --- Standard}
 @tab Failure is visible, contained, and recoverable by re-running the tool.
Most CLIs, inspectors, and reporters.
@item
@strong{D --- Incidental}
 @tab Failure has no consequence beyond the session. Skills, themes,
documentation tooling, scratch utilities, and projects registered as games
(§18.5).
@end multitable

@strong{Silent wrongness raises the category.} A tool whose wrong answer looks
exactly like a right answer is Category B at minimum, whatever its size. A
prayer-time calculator, a checksum reporter, and a specification extractor all
qualify: nothing downstream can detect the error.

@strong{One category per project, raised per subsystem.} A project declares one
category. An individual subsystem MAY be raised above it --- a Category C
application that ships a credential-handling module treats that module as
Category A --- and MUST NOT be lowered below it. The raise is recorded with the
declaration.

@node Category Declaration
@section §19.2 — Declaring and Changing a Category

@multitable {Where declared} {@file{README.md} posture section (§5.2), @file{AGENTS.md} (§5.7), and the project registry in @file{PROJECTS.md}. All three carry the same value; the registry is authoritative in a conflict.}
@headitem
Rule
 @tab Detail
@item
Where declared
 @tab The @file{README.md} posture section (§5.2), @file{AGENTS.md} (§5.7), and
the project registry in @file{PROJECTS.md}. All three carry the same value; the
registry is authoritative in a conflict.
@item
Declared before code
 @tab The category is declared at project creation, before the first gate
(§19.4). An undeclared project is treated as Category C for audit purposes, and
the missing declaration is itself a finding.
@item
Raising is free
 @tab A category MAY be raised at any time by the maintainer, effective
immediately.
@item
Lowering is gated
 @tab A category MAY be lowered only with a dated justification in the
tailoring register (§19.5) naming what changed about the consequence of
failure. Shipping pressure is not such a change.
@item
Scope changes force review
 @tab Adding a network listener, a credential path, a privileged operation, or
a destructive default to a project requires the category to be re-evaluated in
the same change.
@end multitable

@node Graded Obligations
@section §19.3 — What Each Category Owes

The chapters that follow are written once and applied by category. This table
is the index; each chapter restates its own row normatively.

@multitable {§21 Traceability matrix in CI} {Test + analysis + formal or exhaustive fuzzing} {Trust-path dependencies} {List in @file{AGENTS.md}} {Recommended}
@headitem
Obligation
 @tab A @tab B @tab C @tab D
@item
§20 Requirements specification
 @tab Texinfo SRS @tab Texinfo SRS @tab List in @file{AGENTS.md} @tab None
@item
§21 Traceability matrix in CI
 @tab Required @tab Required @tab Recommended @tab None
@item
§21 Verification evidence
 @tab Test + analysis + formal or exhaustive fuzzing @tab Test + analysis
@tab Test @tab Smoke test
@item
§22 Budgets & margins
 @tab Required @tab Required @tab Recommended @tab None
@item
§23 Interface control document
 @tab Required @tab Required for public interfaces @tab Recommended @tab None
@item
§24 Dependency qualification
 @tab Every direct dependency @tab Trust-path dependencies @tab Audit tooling
only @tab Audit tooling only
@item
§24 SBOM per release
 @tab Required @tab Required @tab Required @tab Recommended
@item
§25 Baselines & anomalies
 @tab Full @tab Full @tab Release baseline only @tab None
@item
§26 @file{SECURITY.md} & support window
 @tab Required @tab Required @tab Required @tab Required
@end multitable

Every obligation marked @emph{Recommended} that is not implemented is recorded
in the tailoring register (§19.5). @emph{None} needs no record.

@node Engineering Gates
@section §19.4 — The Three Gates

ECSS separates a project into staged reviews with defined entry evidence. That
structure is sound and the staffing behind it is not available here, so this
standard compresses it to three gates, each of which is a @strong{checklist a
maintainer runs alone}, not a meeting.

@multitable {@strong{G2 --- Design gate}} {Entry evidence: requirement set passes the §20.4 characteristics gate; each requirement carries an ID, a rationale, and a verification method; the assurance category is declared. Exit: the requirement set is baselined (§25.1).}
@headitem
Gate
 @tab Evidence required to pass
@item
@strong{G1 --- Requirements gate}
 @tab The requirement set passes the §20.4 characteristics gate; every
requirement carries an ID, a rationale, and a declared verification method
(§21.1); the assurance category is declared (§19.2). Exit: the requirement set
is baselined (§25.1).
@item
@strong{G2 --- Design gate}
 @tab Architecture recorded; concurrency decisions and their trade-offs
documented (§3.2); resource budgets declared with initial margins (§22);
interfaces identified and their ICD drafted (§23); dependencies qualified
(§24). Exit: the design and budget baseline.
@item
@strong{G3 --- Release gate}
 @tab Traceability matrix complete with no orphans (§21.3); budget margins
measured and within threshold (§22.3); no open anomaly above the category's
blocking severity (§25.2); release manifest assembled (§25.3); §16 checklist
run. Exit: the release baseline and a signed tag (§6.3).
@end multitable

Gates are per release, not per project lifetime: a project that ships
quarterly passes G1--G3 quarterly, and a change that adds no requirements
re-enters at G2.

@node Tailoring Register
@section §19.5 — Recorded Tailoring

§16 currently permits marking a check @emph{N/A} in the moment of the audit,
which leaves no record and cannot be reviewed later. Tailoring is instead
@strong{written down once and read every time}.

@multitable {Where it lives} {Category A and B projects ship @file{COMPLIANCE.md} at the repository root. Category C and D projects MAY instead carry it as a Compliance section of @file{README.md}.}
@headitem
Rule
 @tab Detail
@item
Where it lives
 @tab Category A and B projects ship @file{COMPLIANCE.md} at the repository
root. Category C and D projects MAY instead carry the register as a
``Compliance'' section of @file{README.md}.
@item
What it records
 @tab One row per tailored clause: the clause, the status
(@code{applied} / @code{tailored} / @code{not-applicable}), a one-line
justification, and an ISO 8601 date (§14).
@item
Silence means applied
 @tab A clause absent from the register is applied in full. The register never
lists what is merely being followed.
@item
Not a waiver mechanism
 @tab Tailoring adjusts @emph{how} a clause is satisfied or records that its
subject does not exist in this project. It never sets aside §3's priority
order, §4 licensing, §6.3 signing, or §6.4 contribution targets.
@item
Reviewed at G3
 @tab Every register entry is re-read at the release gate. An entry whose
justification has expired is removed and the clause applied.
@end multitable

@node Conformance Claim
@section §19.6 — Claiming Conformance

A project claims conformance in @file{README.md}, in one line, naming the
standard version, the category, and whether the claim is full or tailored:

@example
Conforms to The Steelbore Standard v@value{VERSION} --- Category B, tailored
(§22, §23; see COMPLIANCE.md).
@end example

@strong{Full conformance} means every applicable clause is applied and the
register is empty. @strong{Tailored conformance} means the register is
non-empty and current. A claim naming a standard version older than the one in
force is honest and permitted; an absent claim is not a conformance failure,
but an inaccurate one is.

