@end ifnottex

@node Requirements Engineering
@chapter §20 — Requirements Engineering

Code cannot be verified against an intention that was never written down. §17
already assumes a PRD or plan exists and tracks progress against it, but this
standard has never said what such a document contains, how a requirement is
worded, or how one is identified. This chapter supplies that, scaled by
assurance category (§19.3) so that a Category D skill owes nothing and a
Category A bootloader owes a specification.

@menu
* Requirement Information Items::  §20.1 — What is written, and where.
* Verbal Forms::                   §20.2 — shall, should, may, will.
* Requirement Identity::           §20.3 — Identifiers and attributes.
* Characteristics Gate::           §20.4 — The quality gate for requirements.
* Requirement Language::           §20.5 — Wording rules.
* Requirement Pattern::            §20.6 — The house sentence pattern.
@end menu

@node Requirement Information Items
@section §20.1 — What Is Written, and Where

Two documents, distinguished by whose question they answer. Keeping them apart
is what makes validation (§21.5) possible: needs are what the software is
@emph{for}, requirements are what it @emph{does}.

@multitable {@strong{Requirements (SRS)}} {What the software shall do, in verifiable statements, each traceable to a need. Written by the maintainer as implementer. Lives in @file{doc/<project>.texi} under a @code{Requirements} node.}
@headitem
Item
 @tab Content and location
@item
@strong{Needs}
 @tab Why the software exists, who uses it, the operating environment, and the
constraints the maintainer is unwilling to trade away. Written by the
maintainer as user. Two paragraphs is a legitimate length. Lives in
@file{doc/<project>.texi} under a @code{Needs} node, or in @file{README.md} for
Category C.
@item
@strong{Requirements (SRS)}
 @tab What the software shall do, in individually verifiable statements, each
traceable to a need. Written by the maintainer as implementer. Lives in
@file{doc/<project>.texi} under a @code{Requirements} node.
@end multitable

Requirements are authored in Texinfo (§8), not in a parallel Markdown or
office file: they then build to @code{.info}, @code{.html}, and @code{.pdf}
with the rest of the manual, ship in the packages (§8.6), and cannot drift
from the documentation the user reads. Category C projects MAY keep the
requirement list in @file{AGENTS.md} instead, where the agent that implements
against it will actually load it.

@strong{Requirements are not the plan.} @file{TODO}, @file{PLAN}, and the
milestone rows of §17 sequence the work; the requirement set states the
obligation. A requirement is not deleted because it slipped a milestone.

@node Verbal Forms
@section §20.2 — Verbal Forms

Normative weight is carried by the verb, and one verb has exactly one meaning
throughout this standard, every project specification, and every requirement:

@multitable {@code{shall not}} {A binding prohibition. Verifiable, and its violation is a defect.}
@headitem
Form
 @tab Meaning
@item
@code{shall}
 @tab A binding requirement. Verifiable, and its violation is a defect.
@item
@code{shall not}
 @tab A binding prohibition.
@item
@code{should}
 @tab A recommendation. Departure is permitted and is recorded in the
tailoring register (§19.5).
@item
@code{may}
 @tab Permission. Neither obligation nor recommendation.
@item
@code{will}
 @tab A statement of fact or of intent about something outside the
specification's control (the platform, the toolchain, a future release). Never
a requirement.
@end multitable

@strong{@code{must} is retired from normative text.} It reads as obligation but
carries no agreed weight, and this standard has used @code{must},
@code{MUST}, and @code{shall} interchangeably. New and revised normative text
uses the forms above. Existing occurrences are migrated as their sections are
next revised; until then @code{must} and @code{MUST} in this standard are read
as @code{shall}. Capitalisation is not significant: @code{shall} and
@code{SHALL} are the same obligation, and emphasis is carried by
@code{@@strong@{@}} where needed.

@node Requirement Identity
@section §20.3 — Identifiers and Attributes

@strong{Identifier.} @code{<PREFIX>-<TYPE>-<NNN>}, where @code{<PREFIX>} is the
project's short code as registered in @file{PROJECTS.md}, @code{<TYPE>} is
@code{NEED} or @code{SRS}, and @code{<NNN>} is a zero-padded serial:
@code{ZMK-SRS-014}. Identifiers are @strong{permanent and never reused}. A
withdrawn requirement keeps its identifier and is marked withdrawn; reissuing
@code{ZMK-SRS-014} for a different obligation silently invalidates every trace,
commit message, and test that cited it.

@strong{Attributes.} Every requirement carries these, and a requirement missing
any of them does not pass G1:

@multitable {Verification method} {One of the four methods in §21.1, chosen when the requirement is written --- not after the code exists and the easiest evidence is obvious.}
@headitem
Attribute
 @tab Content
@item
Rationale
 @tab Why this is required. One sentence. A requirement whose rationale cannot
be written is a design decision in disguise and belongs in the design record.
@item
Source
 @tab The need it satisfies, an upstream standard, a platform constraint, or a
clause of this standard.
@item
Priority
 @tab @code{mandatory} / @code{expected} / @code{optional}. Drives what MVP
means in §17.
@item
Verification method
 @tab One of the four methods in §21.1, chosen when the requirement is written,
not after the code exists and the cheapest evidence is obvious.
@item
Status
 @tab @code{draft} / @code{baselined} / @code{implemented} / @code{verified} /
@code{withdrawn}. The denominator of the §17 progress figures.
@end multitable

@node Characteristics Gate
@section §20.4 — The Characteristics Gate

Run at G1, against each requirement and then against the set. The nine
individual and five set characteristics are those of ISO/IEC/IEEE 29148:2018;
the definitions below are this standard's own wording.

@multitable {@strong{Conforming}} {States a single obligation. Two obligations joined by @code{and} are two requirements, because they can be separately met and separately failed.}
@headitem
Each requirement is
 @tab Meaning
@item
@strong{Necessary}
 @tab Removing it leaves a real gap. If nothing breaks when it is deleted, it
was documentation of an implementation choice.
@item
@strong{Appropriate}
 @tab Pitched at this project's level --- it constrains what the software does,
not how a function is written.
@item
@strong{Unambiguous}
 @tab Admits one reading. If two readings survive, two people implement two
things.
@item
@strong{Complete}
 @tab Stands alone, without a to-be-decided, a trailing @code{etc.}, or an
appeal to context that lives only in the maintainer's head.
@item
@strong{Singular}
 @tab States one obligation. Two obligations joined by @code{and} are two
requirements: they can be separately met and separately failed.
@item
@strong{Feasible}
 @tab Achievable within the known constraints --- platform, toolchain,
budgets (§22), and the maintainer's actual time.
@item
@strong{Verifiable}
 @tab A declared method (§21.1) can produce a pass or a fail. If no method can,
the requirement is unmeasurable prose.
@item
@strong{Correct}
 @tab An accurate statement of the need it traces to.
@item
@strong{Conforming}
 @tab Uses the §20.2 verbal forms and the §20.6 pattern.
@end multitable

@multitable {@strong{Able to be validated}} {The set as a whole can be shown to satisfy the needs --- there is no need with nothing tracing to it.}
@headitem
The set is
 @tab Meaning
@item
@strong{Complete}
 @tab Covers every stated need, including error, degraded, and shutdown
behaviour, which is where hobby specifications habitually stop.
@item
@strong{Consistent}
 @tab No requirement contradicts another, and one term means one thing
throughout.
@item
@strong{Feasible}
 @tab Satisfiable together, not merely one at a time --- the §3 priority order
is the tie-break when two requirements pull apart.
@item
@strong{Comprehensible}
 @tab Readable in order by someone who was not in the room, which for this
organization means the maintainer in a year and every coding agent that loads
it.
@item
@strong{Able to be validated}
 @tab Every need has at least one requirement tracing to it, so the set can be
shown to serve its purpose.
@end multitable

@node Requirement Language
@section §20.5 — Wording Rules

@itemize
@item
@strong{No unmeasurable adjectives.} @code{fast}, @code{robust},
@code{efficient}, @code{user-friendly}, @code{secure}, @code{modern},
@code{seamless}. Replace each with a figure and a method: not ``startup shall
be fast'' but ``cold start shall complete within 50 ms on the reference
machine, measured per §22.4''.
@item
@strong{No open-ended clauses.} @code{etc.}, @code{and so on},
@code{as appropriate}, @code{including but not limited to}. Each hides an
unbounded obligation nobody can verify.
@item
@strong{No escape hatches.} @code{where possible}, @code{if practical},
@code{as far as reasonable}. Either the obligation binds --- use @code{shall}
--- or it is a recommendation --- use @code{should}.
@item
@strong{No comparatives without a baseline.} @code{faster}, @code{smaller},
@code{better} require a named reference point and a measurement.
@item
@strong{No implementation in a requirement.} Name the observable behaviour, not
the crate, the data structure, or the algorithm. Those belong to the design
record and change without the obligation changing.
@item
@strong{Active voice, named subject.} ``The system shall reject'' --- not ``the
input shall be rejected'', which leaves nobody responsible.
@item
@strong{Positive statement where possible.} A prohibition is verifiable only if
the prohibited condition is observable; prefer stating the required behaviour.
@item
@strong{No rationale inside the requirement.} The rationale is an attribute
(§20.3). Prose reasoning inside a requirement sentence becomes accidentally
binding.
@end itemize

@node Requirement Pattern
@section §20.6 — The House Sentence Pattern

One pattern, so that requirements can be read and diffed quickly:

@example
[<condition>,] the <subject> shall <action> <object> [<constraint>].
@end example

@multitable {@strong{Constrained}} {@code{When NO_COLOR is set in the environment, the resolver shall select the steelbore-mono variant.}}
@headitem
Shape
 @tab Example
@item
@strong{Plain}
 @tab @code{The resolver shall report the resolved theme and the deciding source under --verbose.}
@item
@strong{Conditional}
 @tab @code{When NO_COLOR is set in the environment, the resolver shall select the steelbore-mono variant.}
@item
@strong{Constrained}
 @tab @code{The tracer shall convert a 4000 x 4000 pixel raster within 2 s on
the reference machine.}
@item
@strong{Prohibition}
 @tab @code{The application shall not write to a path outside the directories
named in §9.}
@end multitable

@iftex
@bigskip@hrule@bigskip
@end iftex
@ifnottex
------------------------------------------------------------------------
@end ifnottex

@node Verification and Validation
@chapter §21 — Verification, Validation & Traceability

§3.1 requires stability to be ``verified by testing''. That is one method of
four, and it says nothing about what evidence is enough, or about the link
between a requirement and the test that discharges it. This chapter separates
verification (was it built right) from validation (was the right thing built),
names the admissible methods, and makes the link between requirement and
evidence machine-checkable.

@menu
* Verification Methods::        §21.1 — The four admissible methods.
* Verification Evidence::       §21.2 — Evidence by category.
* Traceability::                §21.3 — The matrix and its gate.
* Independence Surrogates::     §21.4 — Independence without a second engineer.
* Validation::                  §21.5 — Validating against needs.
@end menu

@node Verification Methods
@section §21.1 — The Four Admissible Methods

Every requirement declares exactly one primary method at G1 (§20.3). Naming the
method up front prevents the familiar retrofit in which whatever evidence
happened to be cheap is declared sufficient.

@multitable {@strong{Review of design}} {Reasoning over the artifact rather than execution: static analysis, model checking, proof, complexity or worst-case bounds, exhaustive enumeration of a small state space.}
@headitem
Method
 @tab What it is, and when it is the right choice
@item
@strong{Test}
 @tab Executing the software against defined inputs and comparing to expected
results. The default for behavioural requirements.
@item
@strong{Analysis}
 @tab Reasoning over the artifact rather than running it: static analysis,
model checking, proof, worst-case bounds, exhaustive enumeration of a small
state space. The right method for concurrency invariants and resource bounds,
which tests sample but do not establish.
@item
@strong{Inspection}
 @tab Direct examination of the artifact for a property visible without
execution: an SPDX header (§4.3), a compiler flag (§3.2.1), a file's line
endings (§6.5), the presence of a required file.
@item
@strong{Review of design}
 @tab Structured reading of the design against the requirement, for properties
that exist only at design level --- privilege separation, failure containment,
the absence of a global lock on a hot path.
@end multitable

A requirement may cite a secondary method as supporting evidence. It may not
cite ``manual check'' as a method: manual checking is inspection, and it is
recorded as such, with what was inspected and when.

@node Verification Evidence
@section §21.2 — Evidence by Category

Coverage figures are a floor for the paths that matter, not a target to farm.
Percentages are line coverage of the crate's own code, excluding generated and
vendored sources.

@multitable {@strong{A --- Critical}} {Everything in B, plus formal verification (Kani, Creusot, or SPARK) or exhaustive fuzzing of every parser and every trust boundary; property tests on all invariants; a documented worst-case bound for every unbounded loop and allocation.}
@headitem
Category
 @tab Minimum evidence at G3
@item
@strong{A --- Critical}
 @tab Everything required of B, plus formal verification (Kani, Creusot, or
SPARK for an Ada component) or exhaustive fuzzing of every parser and trust
boundary; property-based tests over stated invariants; a documented worst-case
bound for every unbounded loop and every allocation on a critical path; zero
@code{unsafe} without a written safety argument at each site.
@item
@strong{B --- Significant}
 @tab Unit and integration tests with at least 80% line coverage; fuzzing of
every input parser; a mutation-testing run (@code{cargo-mutants}) whose
surviving mutants are triaged and either killed or justified; every anomaly
closed by a regression test (§25.2).
@item
@strong{C --- Standard}
 @tab Unit tests over the public surface, integration coverage of the primary
CLI paths, and a CI run that gates merge.
@item
@strong{D --- Incidental}
 @tab A smoke test proving the artifact loads, parses, or renders.
@end multitable

@strong{Coverage is not evidence of verification.} It shows which lines ran,
not which requirements were discharged; the traceability matrix is what shows
that. Mutation score is the honest companion figure for Category A and B, since
it measures whether the tests would notice a defect at all.

@node Traceability
@section §21.3 — Traceability

Traceability shall be @strong{bidirectional} and @strong{mechanically
generated}: forward from each requirement to the design element, the code, and
the evidence that discharges it; backward from each test to the requirement it
exists for. A matrix maintained by hand is a matrix that is accurate on the day
it is written.

@strong{Markers.} Requirements are cited in source by identifier, in a form the
language's own tooling preserves:

@verbatim
/// Resolve the active theme from the §11.6 precedence chain.
///
/// Verifies: SBT-SRS-031, SBT-SRS-032
pub fn resolve_theme(env: &Env) -> Theme { /* ... */ }

#[test]
fn mono_variant_wins_when_no_color_set() {
    // Verifies: SBT-SRS-032
}
@end verbatim

@strong{Generation and gate.} A project script --- Nushell is the house shell
(§7) and its structured output suits this directly --- collects the markers,
joins them against the requirement set, and emits the matrix as a build
artifact:

@verbatim
# tools/trace.nu — emit the traceability matrix, fail on orphans
def main [] {
  let reqs = (open doc/requirements.toml | get requirement)
  let marks = (
    rg --json '(?:Verifies|Implements): ([A-Z]+-SRS-[0-9]+)' src tests
    | lines | each { |l| $l | from json } | where type == "match"
  )
  let covered = ($marks | get data.submatches.0.match.text | uniq)
  let orphan_reqs = ($reqs | where id not-in $covered | get id)
  let unknown_ids = ($covered | where $it not-in ($reqs | get id))

  if ($orphan_reqs | is-not-empty) or ($unknown_ids | is-not-empty) {
    print $"unverified: ($orphan_reqs)  unknown: ($unknown_ids)"
    exit 1
  }
}
@end verbatim

@multitable {Orphans block release} {A baselined requirement with no evidence marker, or a marker citing an identifier that is not in the requirement set, fails CI. These are the two failure modes traceability exists to catch: work that was specified and never done, and work that cites a requirement that was withdrawn or mistyped.}
@headitem
Rule
 @tab Detail
@item
Generated, never hand-maintained
 @tab The matrix is produced by tooling from the source and the requirement
set, on every CI run.
@item
Orphans block release
 @tab A baselined requirement with no evidence marker, or a marker citing an
identifier absent from the requirement set, fails CI. These are the two failure
modes traceability exists to catch: work that was specified and never done, and
evidence citing a requirement that was withdrawn or mistyped.
@item
Shipped as an artifact
 @tab The matrix accompanies the release (§25.3), so the state of verification
at that version is recoverable later without rebuilding.
@item
Feeds §17
 @tab The verified fraction of the baselined requirement set is the
denominator-backed figure behind the §17.1 progress bars.
@end multitable

@node Independence Surrogates
@section §21.4 — Independence Without a Second Engineer

Space software practice requires verification to be performed independently of
the people who wrote the code, in proportion to criticality. A single
maintainer cannot staff that, and pretending otherwise would make the clause
decorative. Independence is therefore obtained from a @strong{different
mechanism}, not a different person, for Category A and B:

@itemize
@item
@strong{A different tool decides.} Formal verification, a model checker, or a
sanitizer run reaches its verdict without sharing the author's assumptions.
@item
@strong{A different implementation decides.} Differential testing against a
reference implementation, an oracle, or the tool being replaced ---
particularly available to Ironway, Caliper, and any rewrite, where the original
is the oracle.
@item
@strong{Generated inputs decide.} Property-based tests and fuzzing explore the
input space the author did not think of, which is the specific failure
independent review is meant to catch.
@item
@strong{A different reader decides.} A review pass by a coding agent that has
not seen the implementation session, prompted from the requirement text alone,
is a legitimate inspection --- recorded, like any inspection, with what was
reviewed and when.
@end itemize

Mutation testing is the meta-check on all of the above: it measures whether the
verification would have noticed.

@node Validation
@section §21.5 — Validation Against Needs

Verification asks whether the software meets its requirements. Validation asks
whether the requirements were the right ones, and is answered against the needs
(§20.1), by using the software as the user for whom it was written --- which,
under §5.1, is usually the maintainer.

@multitable {Validation is per release} {Performed at G3, against the release candidate, in the real environment (Steelbore OS Bravais for OS-facing work), not in the development tree with the developer's configuration.}
@headitem
Rule
 @tab Detail
@item
Against needs, not requirements
 @tab Each stated need is exercised end to end. A need with no requirement
tracing to it is a §20.4 set-completeness failure found late.
@item
In the real environment
 @tab Performed at G3 against the release candidate on the target platform,
installed from the packaging of §5.5 --- not from the development tree with the
developer's configuration.
@item
Recorded
 @tab A short validation note in the release manifest (§25.3): what was
exercised, on what, and what was found.
@item
Failure is a requirements defect
 @tab Software that passes verification and fails validation has correct code
and wrong requirements. The fix begins in the requirement set, not the source.
@end multitable

@iftex
@bigskip@hrule@bigskip
@end iftex
@ifnottex
------------------------------------------------------------------------
@end ifnottex

@node Resource Budgets
@chapter §22 — Resource Budgets & Margins

§3.2 requires benchmarking before and after optimization work. A before/after
delta detects a regression against the previous build; it cannot detect a build
that has been slowly getting worse for a year, and it never answers whether the
current figure is acceptable. A budget supplies the absolute line, and a margin
says how much room is left before it is crossed.

@menu
* Budgeted Resources::          §22.1 — What is budgeted.
* Declaring Budgets::           §22.2 — Declaration and re-baselining.
* Margin Thresholds::           §22.3 — Green, amber, red.
* Budget Measurement::          §22.4 — The reference machine.
@end menu

@node Budgeted Resources
@section §22.1 — What Is Budgeted

At G2, a Category A or B project declares a ceiling for each resource that
applies to it. Category C should declare at least the first two.

@multitable {Cold-start latency} {Time from process start to the first useful output or interactive prompt, measured with @code{hyperfine} on a warm page cache.}
@headitem
Resource
 @tab Definition
@item
Binary size
 @tab Size of the stripped release artifact as shipped by §5.5 packaging.
@item
Cold-start latency
 @tab Time from process start to first useful output or interactive prompt,
measured on a warm page cache.
@item
Peak resident memory
 @tab Maximum RSS over the declared reference workload.
@item
Throughput or frame time
 @tab The project's primary work rate: pixels per second, packets per second,
frames per second, requests per second.
@item
Startup allocations
 @tab Allocation count before first output, for Category A --- an allocation on
a path that must not fail is a failure mode.
@item
Hard platform limits
 @tab Where the target imposes one --- a boot sector, a flash partition, a
stack size --- the limit is the ceiling and is not negotiable. Zamak is the
standing example.
@end multitable

@node Declaring Budgets
@section §22.2 — Declaration and Re-Baselining

@itemize
@item
Budgets are declared in the requirement set (§20.1) as ordinary requirements
with figures, so they are verified and traced like any other.
@item
A budget is declared @strong{before} the code that consumes it, at G2. A
ceiling set after measurement is a description, not a budget.
@item
A ceiling MAY be re-baselined. Re-baselining requires a dated entry recording
the old ceiling, the new one, and what changed to justify it --- a new feature,
a platform change, a deliberate trade. Re-baselining to accommodate an
unexplained regression is prohibited: find the regression first.
@item
Every budget names the reference machine and the reference workload (§22.4). A
figure without both is not reproducible and cannot be compared across releases.
@end itemize

@node Margin Thresholds
@section §22.3 — Margin Thresholds

Margin is the headroom remaining: @code{(ceiling - measured) / ceiling}.

@multitable {@strong{Amber --- 10% to 25%}} {Recorded in the release manifest with a one-line note. The project is close enough to the ceiling that the next feature will cross it, and that is now visible rather than discovered at the crossing.}
@headitem
Band
 @tab Consequence
@item
@strong{Green --- above 25%}
 @tab Normal. Reported, no action.
@item
@strong{Amber --- 10% to 25%}
 @tab Recorded in the release manifest with a one-line note: the next feature
will cross the ceiling, and that is now visible rather than discovered at the
crossing.
@item
@strong{Red --- below 10%}
 @tab Blocks the release gate for Category A and B. Clear it by reducing
consumption or by re-baselining the ceiling under §22.2, never by suppressing
the measurement.
@item
@strong{Exceeded --- negative}
 @tab A defect, filed as an anomaly (§25.2) at the severity the resource
warrants. For a hard platform limit it is always S1.
@end multitable

@strong{Margins are reported, not just measured.} Where a project reports
progress under §17, the margin band accompanies it, so that erosion is visible
in the same block as completion:

@example
BUDGET: binary 3.4 MiB / 4.0 MiB (15% margin, amber)
@end example

@node Budget Measurement
@section §22.4 — The Reference Machine and Workload

@multitable {Reference workload} {A fixed, committed input --- a specific raster for Caliper, a specific save file for Ironway, a specific configuration for Pearlite --- versioned with the project so that a figure from one release is comparable with the next.}
@headitem
Element
 @tab Requirement
@item
Reference machine
 @tab Named in the requirement set: CPU model, core count, memory, storage
class, and OS. Where CI is the reference, the runner class is named and pinned.
@item
Reference workload
 @tab A fixed, committed input --- a specific raster for Caliper, a specific
save for Ironway, a specific configuration for Pearlite --- versioned with the
project so a figure from one release is comparable with the next.
@item
Automated
 @tab Measurement runs in CI on every release build. A budget checked by hand
is a budget checked once.
@item
Tools
 @tab @code{criterion} for throughput, @code{hyperfine} for wall-clock start,
@code{cargo bloat} and the stripped artifact size for binary budgets,
@code{/usr/bin/time -v} or an in-process allocator hook for peak RSS.
@item
Noise discipline
 @tab Report the median and the spread, not a single run, and treat a change
inside the spread as no change.
@end multitable

@iftex
@bigskip@hrule@bigskip
@end iftex
