------------------------------------------------------------------------
@end ifnottex

@node Interface Control
@chapter §23 — Interface Control

Everything this standard says about compatibility today is implicit: §5.3 asks
general-use projects for semantic versioning, and semantic versioning is a
promise about an interface nobody has written down. An interface that was never
enumerated cannot be versioned, deprecated, or kept --- it is discovered by the
consumer at the moment it breaks. This chapter enumerates it.

@menu
* What Is an Interface::        §23.1 — The interface inventory.
* Interface Control Document:: §23.2 — The ICD.
* Compatibility Policy::       §23.3 — Changing an interface.
@end menu

@node What Is an Interface
@section §23.1 — The Interface Inventory

Every project maintains an inventory of the interfaces it exposes. The
inventory is short and it is exhaustive; an interface omitted from it is an
interface that will be broken by accident.

@multitable {Environment variables} {Names the project reads or sets --- @code{SPACECRAFT_THEME}, @code{NO_COLOR}, @code{SPACECRAFT_A11Y}. Reading a variable is a contract with whoever sets it.}
@headitem
Interface class
 @tab What is controlled
@item
Library API
 @tab Public items of a crate or shared library: types, functions, traits,
public fields, and the error types they return.
@item
Command-line surface
 @tab Subcommand and option names, argument order, exit codes, and the shape of
@code{--json} or @code{--format} output. Governed in detail by the Dual-Mode
Self-Documenting CLI Standard; enumerated here because it is an interface.
@item
Machine-readable output
 @tab Any output another program parses. It carries a schema and a schema
version, and the schema is committed.
@item
On-disk and configuration formats
 @tab File formats the project reads or writes, including
@file{/etc/steelbore/theme.toml} (§11.6.4), state files, caches with a stable
location, and save formats.
@item
Wire and IPC protocols
 @tab Sockets, pipes, message formats, and their framing.
@item
Environment variables
 @tab Names the project reads or sets --- @code{SPACECRAFT_THEME},
@code{NO_COLOR}, @code{SPACECRAFT_A11Y}. Reading a variable is a contract with
whoever sets it.
@item
Filesystem layout
 @tab Paths the project creates, requires, or documents, and the XDG bases it
resolves them against (§9).
@end multitable

@node Interface Control Document
@section §23.2 — The Interface Control Document

Category A and B projects ship an ICD; Category C should. It lives as a
@code{@@node Interfaces} chapter of @file{doc/<project>.texi} (§8) rather than
as a separate file, so it builds and ships with the manual.

@multitable {Machine-readable schema} {Where the interface is data --- JSON output, a TOML configuration, a wire message --- a machine-readable schema is committed alongside the prose and is the artifact the tests validate against.}
@headitem
The ICD records
 @tab Detail
@item
Identity and version
 @tab Each interface has a name and its own version, which need not track the
project version. An interface version changes only when the interface does.
@item
Contract
 @tab Names, types, ranges, units (§14 metric), encodings (§6.5 UTF-8), and
required ordering.
@item
Error behaviour
 @tab What the interface does with invalid input, and what the consumer may
rely on: exit codes, error types, partial-write guarantees.
@item
Stability class
 @tab @code{stable} / @code{unstable} / @code{internal}. Unstable and internal
interfaces are documented as such at the point of use; an interface with no
declared class is stable by default, which is the safe assumption for the
consumer and the expensive one for the maintainer.
@item
Machine-readable schema
 @tab Where the interface is data --- JSON output, a TOML configuration, a wire
message --- a schema is committed alongside the prose and is what the tests
validate against.
@item
Consumers
 @tab Known first-party consumers, so the blast radius of a change is visible
before the change.
@end multitable

@node Compatibility Policy
@section §23.3 — Changing an Interface

@multitable {Deprecate before removing} {A stable interface element is marked deprecated for at least one minor release before removal, warns at run time once per invocation, and names its replacement. Removal without a deprecation period is a breaking change even if the element was unused.}
@headitem
Rule
 @tab Detail
@item
Additive changes are minor
 @tab A new subcommand, a new optional field, a new variant behind an
exhaustive-match guard.
@item
Breaking changes are major
 @tab Removing or renaming an element, tightening accepted input, changing a
default, changing an exit code, or changing the meaning of an existing field.
Changing a default is the one most often mistaken for a minor change.
@item
Deprecate before removing
 @tab A stable element is marked deprecated for at least one minor release
before removal, warns once per invocation at run time, and names its
replacement (§26.3).
@item
Frozen at G3
 @tab The ICD is baselined at the release gate (§25.1). An interface change
after the freeze re-enters at G2.
@item
Recorded in @file{CHANGELOG.md}
 @tab Every interface change appears in the changelog under the version that
made it, in the consumer's terms rather than the implementation's.
@end multitable

@iftex
@bigskip@hrule@bigskip
@end iftex
@ifnottex
------------------------------------------------------------------------
@end ifnottex

@node Reuse Qualification
@chapter §24 — Reuse & Third-Party Qualification

§4.2 and §4.3 settle the @emph{legal} question about code this organization did
not write: preserve the notices, ship the licence, tag the file. They say
nothing about the @emph{engineering} question, which is whether the dependency
is fit to be trusted with the stability, performance, and security priorities of
§3. A crate that is perfectly licensed and unmaintained is compliant today and a
Priority 1 defect next year.

@menu
* Qualification Record::        §24.1 — What is recorded per dependency.
* Qualification Gates::         §24.2 — When a dependency may be adopted.
* Software Bill of Materials::  §24.3 — SBOM per release.
@end menu

@node Qualification Record
@section §24.1 — The Qualification Record

Recorded in @file{DEPENDENCIES.md} at the repository root, or as a
@code{@@node Dependencies} chapter of the manual. Category A records every
direct dependency; Category B records those in the trust path --- anything
parsing untrusted input, handling credentials, touching the filesystem with
privilege, or on a critical path; Category C and D rely on the tooling of
§24.2 alone.

@multitable {Unsafe posture} {Volume and location of @code{unsafe} in the dependency (@code{cargo geiger}), and whether the crate's own soundness is load-bearing for ours.}
@headitem
Field
 @tab Content
@item
Purpose
 @tab What it does for this project, in one line, and what would have to be
written by hand without it.
@item
Provenance
 @tab Upstream repository, maintainer or organization, and the licence as
recorded under §4.2.
@item
Maintenance
 @tab Last release date, release cadence, open-issue posture, and whether the
project is archived or has a stated successor.
@item
Security history
 @tab Past advisories (RUSTSEC or equivalent), and how upstream handled them.
@item
Unsafe posture
 @tab Volume and location of @code{unsafe} (@code{cargo geiger}), and whether
the dependency's soundness is load-bearing for ours.
@item
Transitive weight
 @tab Count of transitive dependencies pulled in, and any duplicate versions
introduced (@code{cargo tree --duplicates}).
@item
Alternatives considered
 @tab What else was available, and why this one.
@item
Exit plan
 @tab What happens if it is abandoned: vendor it under §4.2, replace it, or
absorb the functionality. Written before adoption, when the answer is cheap.
@end multitable

@node Qualification Gates
@section §24.2 — Adopting a Dependency

@multitable {Unqualified in Category A} {Software that cannot be qualified --- closed source, no source archive, no licence, or unauditable --- shall not appear in a Category A path, and shall not be introduced into a Category B path without a tailoring-register entry (§19.5).}
@headitem
Rule
 @tab Detail
@item
Audit before adoption
 @tab @code{cargo audit} and @code{cargo deny} (licence, advisory, source, and
duplicate policy) run before the dependency enters the lockfile, and in CI
thereafter --- extending the §3.3 rule from a one-time check to a standing gate.
@item
Pinned and locked
 @tab The lockfile is committed, and the toolchain is pinned
(@file{rust-toolchain.toml}, @file{flake.lock}) so that a build is reproducible
from the baseline (§25.1).
@item
Vendoring is a first-class option
 @tab Where upstream is unmaintained or the patch is small, carrying it
in-tree under §4.2 is preferred to depending on a repository nobody is
watching, and to the §6.4 outbound submission that upstreaming would require.
@item
Unqualified software in Category A
 @tab Software that cannot be qualified --- unavailable source, no licence,
unauditable, or unbuildable from source --- shall not appear in a Category A
path, and shall not enter a Category B path without a tailoring-register entry
(§19.5).
@item
Re-qualified at G3
 @tab The record is re-read at each release gate. A dependency that has gone
quiet since the last release is a finding, not a footnote.
@end multitable

@node Software Bill of Materials
@section §24.3 — Software Bill of Materials

Every release of a Category A, B, or C project ships an SBOM.

@itemize
@item
Format: CycloneDX or SPDX, generated by tooling (@code{cargo cyclonedx},
@code{cargo sbom}) --- never assembled by hand.
@item
Generated from the same locked inputs as the release artifacts, in the same CI
run, so the two cannot disagree.
@item
Shipped as a release asset with a SHA-256 checksum, listed in the release
manifest (§25.3), and covered by the §5.5 packaging where the format permits.
@item
An advisory published under §26.2 cites the affected releases by SBOM, so
consumers can answer whether they are affected without reading a diff.
@end itemize

@iftex
@bigskip@hrule@bigskip
@end iftex
@ifnottex
------------------------------------------------------------------------
@end ifnottex

@node Configuration and Releases
@chapter §25 — Baselines, Anomalies & Release Acceptance

A release is currently defined in this standard by what accompanies it --- three
package definitions (§5.5), signed commits (§6.3), a manual (§8). This chapter
defines what a release @emph{is}: a frozen configuration, a known defect state,
and an assembled manifest that a future maintainer can reconstruct.

@menu
* Baselines::                   §25.1 — What a baseline freezes.
* Anomalies::                   §25.2 — Classification and closure.
* Release Manifest::            §25.3 — Acceptance contents.
@end menu

@node Baselines
@section §25.1 — Baselines

A baseline is a named, frozen set of artifacts taken at a gate (§19.4). It is
what ``the same build'' means six months later.

@multitable {Toolchain and environment} {Compiler and toolchain pins (@file{rust-toolchain.toml}), the flake or channel revision (@file{flake.lock}), and the build flags applied and disabled per §3.2.1.}
@headitem
A baseline freezes
 @tab Detail
@item
Source
 @tab The commit identifier, signed per §6.3 and tagged at the release
baseline.
@item
Dependencies
 @tab The committed lockfile and the SBOM generated from it (§24.3).
@item
Toolchain and environment
 @tab Toolchain pins (@file{rust-toolchain.toml}), the flake or channel
revision (@file{flake.lock}), and the build flags applied and disabled per
§3.2.1.
@item
Requirements
 @tab The requirement set at its baselined status (§20.3), and the
traceability matrix generated against it (§21.3).
@item
Budgets
 @tab Declared ceilings and measured margins (§22).
@item
Interfaces
 @tab The ICD and its committed schemas (§23.2).
@end multitable

Three baselines exist per release, one per gate: requirements (G1), design and
budgets (G2), release (G3). Nothing is re-cut silently: changing a baselined
artifact means re-entering at that gate.

@node Anomalies
@section §25.2 — Anomalies

An anomaly is any observed departure from a requirement, a budget, an
interface contract, or documented behaviour --- including one found by the
maintainer before anyone else sees it. Anomalies are recorded in the project's
issue tracker under the authorized namespaces of §6.4, never in a private note.

@multitable {@strong{S2 --- Major}} {A requirement is not met, an interface contract is violated, or a budget ceiling is exceeded, with a workaround available. Blocks release for Category A and B unless recorded with a dated justification.}
@headitem
Severity
 @tab Definition and release consequence
@item
@strong{S1 --- Critical}
 @tab Data loss or corruption, a security or privacy breach, an unbootable or
unusable system, or a wrong answer that cannot be detected downstream.
@strong{Blocks release in every category.}
@item
@strong{S2 --- Major}
 @tab A requirement is not met, an interface contract is violated, or a budget
ceiling is exceeded, with a workaround available. Blocks release for Category A
and B unless carried with a dated justification in the manifest.
@item
@strong{S3 --- Minor}
 @tab Incorrect behaviour with a straightforward workaround, or a cosmetic
defect in a documented surface. Carried in known issues.
@item
@strong{S4 --- Cosmetic}
 @tab No functional effect. Carried indefinitely without ceremony.
@end multitable

@multitable {Closed by a regression test} {An S1 or S2 anomaly is closed only when a test that fails on the defective build and passes on the fix is committed, cited by anomaly identifier. Closing by inspection permits the same defect to return unnoticed.}
@headitem
Rule
 @tab Detail
@item
Severity is assigned by consequence
 @tab Not by how hard the fix is, and not by how embarrassing the defect is.
@item
Closed by a regression test
 @tab An S1 or S2 is closed only when a test that fails on the defective build
and passes on the fix is committed, citing the anomaly identifier. Closing by
inspection permits the same defect to return unnoticed.
@item
Traced to a requirement
 @tab Where an anomaly reveals that no requirement covered the behaviour, the
requirement set is amended (§20) --- the anomaly found a specification gap, not
only a bug.
@item
Known issues ship
 @tab Open S2--S4 anomalies are listed in the release manifest and in the
release notes. A defect the maintainer knows about and the user discovers is a
trust failure, not a support event.
@end multitable

@node Release Manifest
@section §25.3 — Release Acceptance and the Manifest

A release is accepted when the G3 evidence exists and the manifest is
assembled. The manifest is committed at the release tag and published with the
release.

@multitable {Verification summary} {Requirements baselined, verified, and withdrawn; the traceability matrix (§21.3); coverage and, for Category A and B, mutation score.}
@headitem
Manifest section
 @tab Contents
@item
Identity
 @tab Project, version, ISO 8601 UTC release timestamp (§14), signed tag and
commit (§6.3), and the assurance category (§19.2).
@item
Artifacts
 @tab Every shipped file with its SHA-256, matching the checksums carried in
the three package definitions (§5.5).
@item
Provenance
 @tab The SBOM (§24.3), toolchain pins, and a reproducibility statement: how
to rebuild, and whether the build is bit-reproducible.
@item
Verification summary
 @tab Requirements baselined, verified, and withdrawn; the traceability matrix
(§21.3); coverage and, for Category A and B, mutation score.
@item
Validation note
 @tab What was exercised at G3, on what platform, and what was found (§21.5).
@item
Budgets
 @tab Each ceiling, its measured value, and its margin band (§22.3).
@item
Known issues
 @tab Open anomalies by severity, with identifiers (§25.2).
@item
Conformance
 @tab The §19.6 claim and the tailoring register state at release.
@end multitable

@strong{Installation is verified, not assumed.} Before the tag is pushed, the
release is installed from each of the three package definitions of §5.5 into a
clean environment, run once, and uninstalled. A package that builds is not a
package that installs.

