------------------------------------------------------------------------
@end ifnottex

@node Operations and Maintenance
@chapter §26 — Operations, Maintenance & Support

This standard governs software up to the moment it ships and then stops. Every
project it governs, however, has a life after the tag: vulnerabilities are
reported, interfaces are retired, and projects end. §5.1 is honest that the
posture is hobby pace with no service-level commitment, and that honesty is
precisely why the terms have to be written down --- a user cannot calibrate
against an unstated expectation.

@menu
* Security Response::           §26.1 — SECURITY.md and reporting.
* Advisories::                  §26.2 — Publishing.
* Deprecation::                 §26.3 — Retiring an interface.
* Support and End of Life::     §26.4 — Windows and archival.
@end menu

@node Security Response
@section §26.1 — @file{SECURITY.md}

§3.3 requires security by design and §5.2 lists the files every project ships,
but no file tells a finder how to report what they found. Every project ships
@file{SECURITY.md} at its root, added to the §5.2 required set.

@multitable {Acknowledgement target} {An honest figure, stated in days and consistent with hobby pace (§5.1). A modest target that is met beats an ambitious one that is not.}
@headitem
@file{SECURITY.md} states
 @tab Detail
@item
Reporting channel
 @tab The private channel: the platform's private vulnerability reporting where
available, otherwise @code{Mohamed.Hammad@@SpacecraftSoftware.org} (§15), with a
public key where one is offered. Never a public issue tracker.
@item
Acknowledgement target
 @tab An honest figure, stated in days and consistent with hobby pace (§5.1).
A modest target that is met beats an ambitious one that is not.
@item
Scope
 @tab Which projects and which versions are in scope, and what is out of scope.
@item
Supported versions
 @tab Which versions receive fixes (§26.4), so a reporter knows whether their
version is covered before they invest the effort.
@item
Coordinated disclosure
 @tab The embargo the maintainer will honour, and the point at which the
finding is published regardless of fix status.
@item
Credit
 @tab Whether the reporter is credited, and where (§15.3).
@end multitable

Security anomalies are S1 by default under §25.2, and their severity is lowered
only with a written argument.

@node Advisories
@section §26.2 — Advisories

@itemize
@item
A fixed vulnerability is published as an advisory on the hosting platform and,
for a published crate, to the ecosystem's advisory database.
@item
The advisory names affected versions, the fixed version, the impact, and the
workaround --- and cites the SBOM (§24.3) so consumers can determine exposure
without reading the diff.
@item
The fix ships as a release under §25.3 like any other, with the anomaly closed
by a regression test (§25.2).
@item
Where the vulnerability came from a dependency, the qualification record
(§24.1) is updated with the incident and upstream's handling of it. That record
is what informs the next adoption decision.
@end itemize

@node Deprecation
@section §26.3 — Deprecation

§5.3 requires general-use projects to have a deprecation policy without saying
what one contains. This is that policy, and it applies to every project that
declares a stable interface (§23.2), general-use or not.

@multitable {Minimum notice period} {One minor release for a Category C or D project; two minor releases or six months, whichever is longer, for Category A and B, whose consumers may be pinned.}
@headitem
Rule
 @tab Detail
@item
Announced in three places
 @tab @file{CHANGELOG.md}, the ICD entry's stability class, and a run-time
warning at the point of use --- once per invocation, on stderr, never
suppressing the operation.
@item
Minimum notice period
 @tab One minor release for Category C and D; two minor releases or six
months, whichever is longer, for Category A and B, whose consumers may be
pinned.
@item
Replacement named
 @tab A deprecation notice that does not say what to use instead is an
abandonment notice.
@item
Removal is major
 @tab Removal lands in a major version (§23.3) and is listed in the release
notes under a breaking-changes heading.
@item
Security exception
 @tab A deprecation forced by a vulnerability may skip the notice period. The
release notes state that it did, and why.
@end multitable

@node Support and End of Life
@section §26.4 — Support Windows and End of Life

@multitable {General-use projects (§5.3)} {The current major version is supported for security fixes for a stated window --- twelve months from the release of its successor is the default --- and the window is stated in @file{README.md} and @file{SECURITY.md}.}
@headitem
Posture
 @tab Support commitment
@item
Personal (§5.1 default)
 @tab Best effort on the latest release only. Stated plainly in
@file{README.md}: no window, no backports, forking encouraged.
@item
General-use (§5.3)
 @tab The current major version receives security fixes for a stated window
--- twelve months from the release of its successor is the default --- stated
in both @file{README.md} and @file{SECURITY.md}.
@end multitable

@strong{End of life is an event with a procedure}, not a repository that stops
receiving commits:

@enumerate
@item
A final release is cut under §25.3, even if it contains no changes, so the last
state is baselined and reproducible.
@item
An EOL notice goes at the top of @file{README.md} with the ISO 8601 date (§14),
the reason, the last supported version, and a successor or fork
recommendation where one exists.
@item
@file{SECURITY.md} is updated to state that no further fixes will be issued.
@item
The §5.5 packaging definitions and the manual stay in the repository: a
downstream packager rebuilding an archived release needs them more than a live
project does.
@item
@file{PROJECTS.md} and the §15.1 subdomain table are updated in the same change,
and the repository is archived on the hosting platform.
@item
Where the project held a reserved codename (§2.1), the registry entry records
the retirement. Codenames are not recycled.
@end enumerate

